{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1486838456,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI3OTE2NjUyNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1282#issuecomment-279166524"},"message":"Question is: do we still need that `if_dirty`? I don't really see it as a given.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1486980651,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI3OTM0NDI4Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1282#issuecomment-279344287"},"message":"Well, a clean repo might be re-obtainable. A dirty one definitely isn't. So, I think a check for this is desirable.\nBTW: Do we currently provide a check on whether or not the current commit would be obtainable from a remote? Might be nice to have, too.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1487011967,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI3OTQ4NTQ0NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1282#issuecomment-279485444"},"message":"the idea of `--kill` was pretty much just to facilitate \"chmod +w -R PATH; rm -rf PATH\" operation, when you do not care to check etc. so none of the dirty/etc checks are of relevance","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1528808018,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE2NzYyMDQzNzU="},"added":["wontfix"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1528809054,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5NjU4MzU3Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1282#issuecomment-396583576"},"message":"I had to \"kill\" quite a number of datasets recently and I want to share this experience: In order to have the desired effect `--kill` would need to be an alias for `-r --nocheck --if-dirty ignore`. In my recent experience I was frequently surprised by the presence of subdatasets that caused a `remove -d \u003cds\u003e --no-check` to fail -- for good reason, in my case.\n\nHowever, given #2655 and the general state of things, maybe a dedicated `--kill` would at least indicate that all bets are off. However, we might want to keep such functionality out of `remove` for clarity otherwise we runs into combinatorial semantics: `--kill` would have to overwrite any other given options, otherwise we would not be able to just run \"chmod +w -R PATH; rm -rf PATH\", but instead would need to go through all the subcontent discovery stack (which is the cause of some latency. But if it overwrites stuff like `-r` people might perform \"unexpected\" removals.\n\nDedicated command (or even a procedure) seems like the better choice.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1528811720,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5NjU5Nzc3MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1282#issuecomment-396597770"},"message":"So you are getting into aliases now? even I am ok without a dedicated kill ;-)","files":null}]}